# Checklist

Work with 3+ steps gets a checklist before its first action. The user follows progress through it, and it holds every step until it is finished. Sub-parts count as steps. Work you realize qualifies partway through still gets a checklist for the remaining steps.

## Tracker
{{- if $taskwarrior }}

The checklist lives in taskwarrior, under the project {{ if $alacritree }}that `{{ $alacritree }} task scope` prints. alacritree shows that project live in its tasks tab.{{ else }}named after the basename of the git root, or of the working directory outside a repo.{{ end }} Use that project as `<scope>`.

| Action | Command |
|--------|---------|
| add | `task rc.verbose=new-uuid add project:<scope> -- '<step>'` prints `Created task <uuid>.` |
| start | `task <uuid> start` |
| finish | `task <uuid> done` |
| drop | `task rc.confirmation=off <uuid> delete` |

Add all steps in one Bash call by chaining the `add` commands with `&&`, so the UUIDs print in step order. Address tasks by UUID, because numeric IDs renumber as tasks complete.
{{ else }}
The checklist lives in the task tool, which pins it above the input.

| Action | Call |
|--------|------|
| add | `TaskCreate` with `subject` (brief title) and `description` (what needs doing), one call per step |
| start | `TaskUpdate` with `status: in_progress` |
| finish | `TaskUpdate` with `status: completed` |
| drop | `TaskUpdate` with `status: deleted` |

In a subagent context with no task tool, track the steps in your replies.
{{- end }}

## Steps

1. Add one task per step, in order, before any other action.
2. For each step, start it, do the work, and finish it once its result is verified. One task is in progress at a time.
3. Add newly discovered work as a task before doing it.
4. Drop a step that turns out unnecessary, and say why in your reply.

The checklist is done when every task in it is finished or dropped.

Keep the checklist in the tracker and report progress in prose, since a list typed into chat scrolls away and can't be updated. If the tracker fails, say so in your reply.
